/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  thermoPhysPropDict (v2) -- T6 dilute-solution (Henry) world.  Henry is a
  CONVENTION for the solutes group, not a model of the whole liquid: the
  solvent sits on the pure-liquid (Raoult) rung, each solute on the
  infinite-dilution rung.  The builder VERIFIES every declared pair file.
  Migrated mechanically from the v1 flat solvent/solutes form (physics
  identical -- the solutes list states the engine's own Henry selection);
  v2 contract 2026-07-17.

  ABOVE Tc (DEV.md 4c C256, measured 2026-11-09).  The `[psatContinued]`
  lines for H2, N2, Ar, NH3 and water are a vapour pressure asked above the
  critical temperature, where the engine continues ln Psat linearly in 1/T.
  The dissolving gases are already on the C256 ladder's first rung (H2, N2
  and Ar are Henry solutes with measured pairs), so the separator's
  dissolved gases and the let-down flash read no continued curve.  Measured
  by scaling the continued Psat (a scratch build): x0.1 moves nothing; x10
  on NH3 ALONE flips the FEHE's cold outlet `preheatedFeed` from a gas at
  669.8 K to a "liquid" at 619.0 K (the converter downstream barely moves:
  the NPV by 5e-5).  The shipped answer, a gas, is the physical one, but it
  holds because the continued NH3 curve is where it is, although every
  component of that stream is above its Tc there (an engine finding,
  reported with C256, not something this file can fix).  A cubic phi-phi
  package for the loop (the ladder's second rung) would decide that phase
  without a vapour pressure, at the price of the measured solubilities in
  the separator; it is left as the case's open question, said here.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components       ( N2  H2  NH3  Ar  water );

equilibrium
{
    formulation diluteSolution;

    liquid
    {
        solvent
        {
            component     NH3;
            standardState pureLiquid;          // the Raoult rung
        }
        solutes
        {
            components    ( N2 H2 Ar );
            standardState infiniteDilution;    // the Henry rung
            solutionModel henryDilute;
            binaryParameters
            {
                N2-NH3 { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/N2-NH3.dat"; }
                H2-NH3 { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/H2-NH3.dat"; }
                Ar-NH3 { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/Ar-NH3.dat"; }
            }
        }
    }

    vapour
    {
        fugacityModel SRK;
    }
}
